Subject: Return-address frames find a function by digest; the field-direct enter hook is opt-in

What this does
  - quickjs.c: every bound bytecode is entered in a registry by its unit digest (rbc_add at bind, rbc_del at unbind and at free), and `rat_bc` looks the registry up before the unit's digest table. A frame of the return-address table whose function the unit holds no entry for (only inlined into others) or whose table slot another bytecode took now still resolves.
  - quickjs.c: the check in `js_aot_content_enter_slow` that breaks a layout when an object enters its shape with its properties outside its own block is compiled only with QJS_AOT_DIRECT_FIELDS, the build that pairs with a unit generated with QJS_AOT_FIELDS_DIRECT=1.

Why we need it
  The backtrace walk (regvm_merge_rat_frames) must name every compiled frame, including those of functions that only exist inlined. The enter check protects a promise only direct field addressing relies on; without that addressing it broke the layout of every object whose properties live out of line and so kept compiled bodies from being entered.

Soundness
  The registry holds borrowed pointers that are removed before the bytecode is freed or rebound; the lookup compares all 16 digest bytes. The enter check is unchanged inside its guard.

Failure modes
  Allocation failure of the registry leaves the old lookup in effect.

Tests
  A compiled unit with an inlined-only function whose frame is on the stack at a backtrace; the Octane richards unit enters (90904 enters in the kit driver) instead of being refused on its entry check.

